1.2.0 (2026-11-24)
- One of the lock-opened moments gets a proper card of its own: a picture from the Pip-Boy's own game data (read only while the card is up), one of the Pip-Boy's own sounds, and a press takes you back to the lock menu. If the holotape is closed before you see it, it shows the next time Lockpick opens.
- Reworded a few older entries below.

1.1.0 (2026-11-20)
- Fixed: the end-of-run card said NEW BEST SCORE! when a run only tied the best score from an earlier run. The save now remembers the best from when the run started, and only a higher score counts as new (a run already under way when you update still uses the old check once).
- One of the lock-opened cards no longer spells out the controls (it says PRESS FOR THE NEXT LOCK); those controls are on the CONTROLS page with the rest.
- Checked in a full scripted playthrough in the test harness: a clean pick at every lock level (pins, score, best and save checked after each), snapping pins down to OUT OF BOBBY PINS, forcing, a new run and a missing scene file; no stale timers, nothing left after closing.
- The startup loader that brings this holotape's hook back after a restart (loader v4) runs each hook with new Function instead of eval. With eval, every hook that stays switched on (the STATS rows, the MISC hand-off, the journal in DATA) kept the loader's working space, and with it the hook's whole source text, in memory for as long as the Pip-Boy was on. Measured in a desktop copy of the Pip-Boy's own JavaScript engine (Espruino 2v29): all our hooks together held about 970 blocks after a restart; now about 170. Nothing in the Pip-Boy's own firmware or menus changes. The first restart after updating still uses the old loader once and replaces it.
- Opening the holotape no longer runs its STATS hook again when the hook is already running and listed for startup. The check looked for an old global (RM_STATS) that the hooks stopped setting, so the hook ran on every open; it now checks RM_PATCH like the other hooks.
- The STATS > GENERAL record rows can no longer be changed with the right wheel. That screen is the Pip-Boy's own editable list and it saves the whole list to SETTINGS/GENERAL.JSON when you leave it, so a turned wheel changed the record shown and saved a copy of our rows into the Pip-Boy's settings. Our rows are now handed to the screen read-only and are left out of that save; the Pip-Boy's own rows still change and save as before, and copies of our rows saved earlier are dropped the next time the screen saves. Checked against the Pip-Boy's own GENERAL screen code in the desktop Espruino simulator.
- New CONTROLS entry at the bottom of the lock menu: a page with every control, so the screens no longer spell them out. The lock menu's hint line, the control list beside the lock and the other hint lines under the locks are gone, leaving the screen to the game.
- The startup loader that keeps the STATS hook working after a restart is quieter and more careful (after Aidan's Lab's review of the firmware-update problem): it never prints or logs anything to the USB console, so it can no longer upset a firmware update or the companion app; it waits for the Pip-Boy's own code (Pip, the screen, the list maker and the settings reader) to finish starting before running any hook, and if that never happens it quietly gives up after a minute instead; it runs only once per start; and errors are kept in RM_HOLO_ERR for the terminal instead.
- Opening the tape upgrades the old loader in place. The loader carries a version, so newer and older hooks no longer take turns rewriting it, and hooks from before v2 are skipped at startup until their tape is updated.
- The hook frees its setup code once it has run, so it holds less memory while the Pip-Boy is on.
- The STATS hook now keeps almost nothing in memory. Instead of every game keeping its own row code loaded all the time (about 30 blocks each on the Pip-Boy), the rows are read from the games' save files only while STATS > GENERAL opens. Hooks from older versions are not run there.
- Run over is now a result card like the other games: Vault Boy picking a lock at the top, OUT OF BOBBY PINS, locks picked and score, best score, and the prompt for a new run.
- The other lock-opened screen uses the same card, with its controls on one line clear of the rounded bottom corners.
- The lock menu has one hint line instead of two (the second sat in the rounded bottom corner). Pressing during a lock still returns to the menu, as the controls beside the lock say.
- Wording: "1 BOBBY PIN LEFT" and "+1 PIN" instead of "1 BOBBY PINS" and "+1 PINS".
- Lockpick lets go of the wheels before another of its screens takes them, so the Pip-Boy no longer logs "Event knob1 not exclusive" warnings each time.
- The bars and hints on one of the lock screens sit clear of the screen's curved right edge and rounded corners.

1.0.1 (2026-11-06)
- Fixed the STATS hook not starting on Pip-Boys whose internal storage is full ("Unable to find or create file" when writing its startup slot). The STATS row now shows anyway; if the startup slot can't be written, opening Lockpick once after a full restart brings it back.
- A full or write-locked SD card no longer crashes the game: a save that cannot be written is skipped.
- If one of Lockpick's files is missing, or memory is too low to load it, Lockpick returns to the lock menu with a notice instead of a screen that ignores the wheels.

1.0.0 (2026-10-18)
https://github.com/CodyTolene/pip-boy-3000-holotapes/pull/121
- Initial release.
- README tidied up.
- While picking, the lock level and controls on the left sit clear of the screen's curved edge and rounded corner.
- The lock details panel only shows details that apply to the lock you are about to pick.
- Your record shows in the Pip-Boy's own STATS > GENERAL screen. The hook ships with the holotape as HOLO/LOCKPICK/STATS_HOOK.JS; it switches on when you open Lockpick and registers itself in one shared boot slot (.boot1-.boot3, never .boot0) so it keeps working after a full restart. No CFW file or Service Records holotape needed.
- The lock menu uses the Pip-Boy's own list style: 14-point rows with the native highlight, like ITEMS, instead of large centred text.
- A details panel beside the list shows the highlighted lock's pin strength, sweet spot and points, using the Pip-Boy's own stat blocks, with your bobby pins, score and best run underneath.
- Header drawn in the Pip-Boy's own layout (title on the line, info underneath), so nothing is cut off by the rounded corners of the screen.
- Icon is now a Pip-Boy .img, so it shows in ITEMS > MISC.
- Draws Vault Boy picking a lock and a real bobby pin from the Pip-Boy's own game data (Fallout 3 or New Vegas mode) on the menu, the lock screen and the run-over screen. Falls back to the plain look if the data is missing.
- Gameplay additions.
- Initial release of Lockpick, with five lock levels, breakable bobby pins, forcing, and saved progress.
- Use only the SCROLL, TAB and SELECT UI sounds, which every firmware provides.
- Each new bobby pin starts at the top of the lock.
- The outer ring of the lock shakes harder the closer the pin is to breaking.
- The right wheel moves the bobby pin and turning the left wheel either way tries the lock.
- Runs: start with 8 pins, earn pins and score by picking locks, and the run ends when the pins run out. Best score is saved.
- Very Easy locks pay score but no pins.
- Fixed an OUT OF MEMORY crash when starting Lockpick from ITEMS > MISC. The Vault Boy and bobby pin art and the STATS hook check now load just after the game opens, once the Pip-Boy has freed the MISC list.
- The STATS hook creates the USER folder when the SD card has none, so it can register for startup instead of failing with NO_PATH.
